<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodulf of Orléans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 750–821 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> was a scholar, poet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, of Visigothic origin. He was one of the leading figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>, promoting education and the correction of religious texts. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> name him, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, among the scholars associated with the palace school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodulf-of-orleans.jpg" alt="Theodulf of Orléans" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manfred Heyde · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germigny_Des_Pres_2007_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
